{"title":"Subsets","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"You are given an array of `n` **distinct** integers. List every **non-empty** subset of the array.\n\nA subset is formed by choosing any group of positions of the array; the chosen elements are always written in the same relative order in which they appear in the array. Two subsets are different if they use different positions.\n\nThe subsets must be printed in a fixed order so the answer is unique: compare subsets by the **sequence of positions** they use, as in dictionary order. A subset whose position sequence is a prefix of another one comes first. For `[a, b, c]` the order is `a`, `a b`, `a b c`, `a c`, `b`, `b c`, `c`.\n\nThe empty subset is **not** listed, so exactly `2^n - 1` subsets are printed.\n\n**Follow-up:** Generate the same subsets without recursion by iterating over bitmasks, and think about how the output size limits the largest `n` that is practical.","inputFormat":"The first line contains an integer `n`.\nThe second line contains `n` space-separated distinct integers.","outputFormat":"Print `2^n - 1` lines. Each line holds one subset: its elements separated by single spaces, in the array's order. Lines appear in the order described above.","constraints":["1 ≤ n ≤ 12","-100 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 100","All values are distinct"],"examples":[{"input":"3\n1 2 3","output":"1\n1 2\n1 2 3\n1 3\n2\n2 3\n3","explanation":"With three elements there are 7 non-empty subsets. Starting from position 0 we get `1`, then extend it: `1 2`, `1 2 3`, then `1 3`. Next the subsets starting at position 1: `2`, `2 3`, and finally `3`."},{"input":"2\n5 -1","output":"5\n5 -1\n-1","explanation":"The subsets are `5`, `5 -1` and `-1`. Elements keep the array order, so `5` comes before `-1` in the middle line, even though `-1` is smaller."},{"input":"1\n9","output":"9","explanation":"A single element has exactly one non-empty subset."}],"tests":[{"input":"1\n7","output":"7"},{"input":"2\n-100 100","output":"-100\n-100 100\n100"},{"in